An approach to estimate CCV (cycle-to-cycle variation) of effective
energy output of thermal engine: A case study on a high speed
gasoline engine
Yangyang Chen a, Banglin Deng b, *, Mingmin Chen b, Kaihong Hou b
a
    School of Mechanical and Automotive Engineering, South China University of Technology, Guangzhou, 510640, Guangdong, China
b
    College of Mechatronics and Control Engineering, Shenzhen University, Shenzhen, 518060, China




A R T I C L E I N F O                                          A B S T R A C T

Keywords:                                                      Cycle-to-cycle variations (CCV) of engine (or other power sources) effective power output is a
Cycle-to-cycle variation                                       crucial parameter for engine itself and connecting machines, but limited by the response char­
Indicated mean effective pressure                              acteristic of measuring instrument, it is still an unattainable parameter. In the current study, an
Brake mean effective pressure
                                                               approach is proposed to estimate the cyclic variations of engine effective power output. We
Internal combustion engine
Friction loss modeling
                                                               designed two experiment scenarios to measure in-cylinder parameters in cyclic level and engine
Transmission accuracy                                          friction loss. Then, friction loss modeling is calibrated by this experimental data, such that in-
                                                               cylinder parameters (especially maximal pressure) and engine operating parameters (such as
                                                               speed) can be integrated into FMEP in cyclic level. Thus, cyclic variation of effective power output
                                                               can be finally deduced. We found that Chen-Flynn friction loss model can well predict FMEP after
                                                               careful calibration. The FMEP keeps synchronously with Pmax against cycle and FMEP generally
                                                               decreases cycle fluctuation of BMEP, the average COV of BMEP is lower than that of IMEP by
                                                               ~33% and ~32% at 60% and 100% load, respectively. CCV of BMEP shows nonsynchronous
                                                               characteristics relative to that of IMEP, this is affected by cycle evolution feature of FMEP. So,
                                                               CCV of in-cylinder parameters cannot be directly used to assess CCV of effective power output.
                                                               Finally, when the CCV output is considered in analysis of connecting machines, the transmission
                                                               accuracy for connecting machines is deteriorated largely, and the vibration and sound will be
                                                               more intense. Thus, the engine CCV output cannot be ignored in assessment of connecting
                                                               machines.




1. Introduction

    Internal combustion engines (ICEs) consume much energy and produce a number of harmful pollution emissions [1,2]. According
to BP’s statistics [3], more than half of oil is consumed by ICEs even till to 2040. China Vehicle Environmental Management Annual
Report (2020) [4] reports that, motor vehicles totally emitted 16.038 million tons pollutants (including HC, CO, NOx and PM (particle
matter)) during 2019 in China, and motorcycles’ emissions occupied 8.1% of total HC emission and 9.7% of CO emission, respectively.
Therefore, sector of motorcycle engine is also important for energy saving and pollutant reduction [5]. Further, operating speed range
of motorcycle’s engines is wider than that of four-wheel cars, and most of them are single cylinder. Thus, strong cycle-to-cycle variation



